WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:06.210
After scaring us senseless in The Conjuring
2, the demonic Nun is back to wreak some more

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:09.550
unholy havoc in a 1950s origin story.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:13.760
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and today I'm going to take you behind the

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.869
scenes of the latest film in The Conjuring
franchise and reveal some freaky facts and

00:00:17.869 --> 00:00:20.699
intriguing secrets about the making of The
Nun.

00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:24.470
If you're finding me for the first time, be
sure to subscribe to get all my regular videos

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:28.000
plus more upcoming Nun and horror videos.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.210
Seeing your name pop up in the credits for
a super-popular horror franchise must be fun,

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:36.680
but four members of The Nun's movie crew made
sure they also got their faces on screen in

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.260
one of the freakiest ways possible!

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:43.790
Director Corin Hardy, cinematographer Maxime
Alexandre, producer Michael Clear, and first

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:48.930
AD Harry Boyd all had casts made of their
faces and those casts were then used to create

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:53.280
death masks which appear on the tombs and
wall during the scenes set in the Abbey's

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:54.280
Tomb Room!

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:58.480
Certainly a rather eerie way to have your
face immortalised on film!

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.770
When it came to getting the right look for
the blood on the Abbey's steps, director Corin

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:06.880
Hardy was so particular that he took the matter
into his own hands.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.170
Hardy grabbed a bucket of fake blood and splashed
it on the steps and added some finishing touches

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:14.060
to give it an even more gruesome and realistic
appearance.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:18.899
But Hardy's bloody work on the movie didn't
end there as he also took time to practise

00:01:18.899 --> 00:01:24.180
spitting blood with his cast to make sure
it worked properly and looked good on camera.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.640
Not content with bringing the supernatural
to life on screen, Hardy says he also had

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:33.170
a real-life ghostly encounter of his own while
filming The Nun in the spooky underground

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.759
tunnels of an abandoned Romanian fort.

00:01:35.759 --> 00:01:40.710
The experience happened during rehearsals
for a sinister scene set in a 200-foot-long

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:41.729
corridor of crosses.

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:45.789
As the cast and crew ran through the scene
in the tunnels, Hardy watched the action on

00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:48.119
a monitor in a dark, isolated room.

00:01:48.119 --> 00:01:51.249
When he entered the room, the director said
hello to what he thought were a couple of

00:01:51.249 --> 00:01:55.190
crew sitting in there, then he sat down with
his back to them and watched the monitor for

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:56.320
a good while.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.409
Happy with how the complicated tracking shot
had gone, Hardy turned around to chat with

00:02:00.409 --> 00:02:03.990
the two men but they were no longer there
and neither were their chairs.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:08.319
To add to the creepiness, there was no way
out of the room other than the one door which

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:09.989
was right next to Hardy!

00:02:09.989 --> 00:02:13.970
Although the director describes himself as
a sceptic deep down, he says he thinks they

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:18.790
were the ghosts of Romanian soldiers 'checking
in on him, making sure he did a good shot.'

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:24.400
Incredibly, given her lead roles in both The
Nun and TV series American Horror Story, Taissa

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:29.920
Farmiga has confessed that she really doesn't
like being scared and so isn't a fan of horror

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:30.920
movies.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:34.989
In fact, despite several attempts, she just
cannot finish watching The Conjuring which

00:02:34.989 --> 00:02:37.010
stars her sister Vera.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:41.090
And when she first watched The Nun, she did
so in a fully lit screening room with her

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:46.879
boyfriend by her side, and even then she often
felt the need to cover her eyes and ears!

00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:50.790
Originally, according to Corin Hardy, he was
looking for an actress from England or elsewhere

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.980
in Europe to play the part of Sister Irene.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:58.250
And, actually, until he saw Taissa's audition
tape, Hardy says he wasn't at all keen on

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:02.610
casting her simply because her sister plays
Lorraine Warren in The Conjuring movies.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:06.830
Funnily enough, Taissa received the script
for The Nun while she was at dinner with Vera.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:11.590
And Vera advised Taissa not to bring work
home with her while filming the movie; but

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:15.780
rather to try to separate herself from all
the stress and the horror and leave that on

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:19.800
set in order to not let the supernatural stuff
get to her.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.420
Taissa used meditation and breathing exercises
to avoid focusing on the fact that at night

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.819
she was all alone in a hotel room in Romania.

00:03:26.819 --> 00:03:31.180
And that did help until many weeks into the
shoot when she 'let [her] guard down' after

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:35.200
filming scenes on the water tank set and then
she did have nightmares.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:41.299
As if the Abbey's blood-spattered steps weren't
quite creepy enough already, production designer

00:03:41.299 --> 00:03:45.819
Jennifer Spence has revealed that some of
the local Romanian artisans she hired to construct

00:03:45.819 --> 00:03:50.569
those huge stone steps were sculptors and
they carved various things into the stone

00:03:50.569 --> 00:03:53.959
including the word "sinners" which the blood
drips into!

00:03:53.959 --> 00:03:58.359
The filmmakers used those steps to create
a new faux entrance at the back of Corvin

00:03:58.359 --> 00:04:02.069
Castle in Romania where these exterior scenes
of the Abbey were shot.

00:04:02.069 --> 00:04:06.909
And the castle owners liked the new addition
so much, including its carefully crafted replicas

00:04:06.909 --> 00:04:11.239
of the castle walls, that they asked the filmmakers
to leave the set behind so tourists could

00:04:11.239 --> 00:04:12.719
visit it.

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:18.019
Curiously, Romanian actress Ingrid Bisu, who
plays Sister Oana, very nearly experienced

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:22.650
real life living in a convent as she considered
becoming a nun when she was younger.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:26.800
And before she started filming The Nun, the
actress visited several monasteries and spent

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.580
hours on end sitting in silence to get herself
in the right head space for her role.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:35.950
On top of that, before shooting a scene, she
often listened to prayers through her earphones.

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:41.560
Believe it or not, if director James Wan hadn't
decided to do reshoots for The Conjuring 2,

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:44.460
the franchise's demonic nun might never have
existed!

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.050
Originally, the film's antagonist was going
to be a horned demon, but after Wan spoke

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:53.360
with the real Lorraine Warren who talked about
being haunted by a hooded figure, the director

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:57.380
came up with the idea of the demonic nun because
he wanted "something that would attack [Lorraine's]

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:58.889
faith" in the movie.

00:04:58.889 --> 00:05:02.840
The reshoots happened a mere three months
before the movie was due to open, and the

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:04.810
rest is horror movie history!

